  "worked_solution": "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $\u27e80,0\u27e9$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$*",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
